I did some more GCC hacking over the holidays and think I've got good news =
for=20
everyone.  Here's the just of the latest Alpha GCC IEEE patch (which is eve=
n=20
smaller than the original *grin*):

1)  Removes -mno-ieee.
2)  Causes the compiler to turn on -mieee if -ffinite-math-only has not bee=
n=20
specified.

I don't believe that one should cause anyone any problems, as no actual gcc=
=20
versions have been compiled with my previous patch (the patch I submitted w=
as=20
included but not enabled).

Two gives everyone what they want (the -ffast-math option turns on the=20
=2D -ffinite-math-only option, so you won't get the -mieee slow down if you=
=20
specify -ffast-math).

So, if you don't want support for NaNs or Infs (i.e. what -mieee enables) t=
urn=20
on -ffinite-math-only (or the larger subset -ffast-math).  I think this mak=
es=20
good sense, as the manual says -ffinite-math only "allow[s] optimizations f=
or=20
floating-point arithmetic that assume that arguments and results are not Na=
Ns=20
or +-Infs"

=2D -T

PS:  The best news is that, while debugging my patch, I discovered that=20
=2D -ffast-math also causes crtfastmath.o to be linked into the resulting=20
executable.  This code sets the FPU to map denormals and underflows to zero=
=20
(i.e. code compiled with -ffast-math does not SIGFPE over denormals).

